      title: "Homer and the Oral Poets",
      body: `## Who was Homer?

The ***Odyssey*** and its companion, the ***Iliad***, are the earliest works of Greek literature, and among the most influential books ever composed. The ancient Greeks attributed both to a poet named **Homer**, about whom they knew almost nothing. Seven cities claimed to be his birthplace. Tradition said he was blind, perhaps because a blind bard, **Demodocus**, appears in the *Odyssey*. Herodotus, writing in the fifth century BCE, thought Homer had lived about four hundred years before his own time, around **850 BCE**. Modern scholars generally date the composition of the poems to the late eighth or early seventh century BCE: the *Iliad* around **750–700 BCE**, and the *Odyssey* a little later, around **725–675 BCE**.

Whether a single poet composed both epics—or either—has been debated since antiquity. Some ancient scholars, called the "**Separatists**" (*chorizontes*), argued that the two poems were by different authors. In **1795**, the German scholar **Friedrich August Wolf**, in his *Prolegomena ad Homerum*, argued that the poems had been composed orally, in separate songs, and stitched together later, after the invention of writing. This began the long debate known as "**the Homeric Question**."

## The world of the poems

The poems are set in the **Bronze Age**, in the generation of the **Trojan War**, a war the Greeks believed had been fought around 1200 BCE between a coalition of Greek kingdoms, led by **Agamemnon** of Mycenae, and the city of **Troy** in northwestern Anatolia. The Mycenaean civilization of that age, with its palaces at Mycenae, Pylos, and Thebes, collapsed around 1200–1100 BCE, and the Greeks lost the art of writing for several centuries—the so-called "**Dark Age**." The memory of the heroic age was preserved in oral poetry, sung by professional bards, and transformed over the centuries, until the poems reached the form we know in the eighth century BCE, around the time the Greeks adopted the **alphabet** from the Phoenicians.

The poems thus combine elements from many periods: Bronze Age weapons and palaces; the iron tools and social customs of later centuries; and the ideals of the aristocratic world of the eighth century, in which they were sung. The world they describe never existed in exactly that form.

## Schliemann and Troy

Was Troy real? In **1870**, the German businessman **Heinrich Schliemann**, convinced of the historical truth of Homer, began to dig at **Hisarlik** in northwestern Turkey, a site the British archaeologist **Frank Calvert** had identified. He found the remains of a series of cities built on top of one another, and a hoard of gold that he called "**Priam's Treasure**," which he smuggled out of the country. (He was wrong about the date; the treasure was a thousand years older than any Trojan War.) Later excavations have shown that a substantial city stood at Hisarlik in the late Bronze Age, and that it was destroyed around **1180 BCE**. Hittite texts mention a city called **Wilusa** in the region, which many scholars identify with Homer's Ilios. Whether a war like the one Homer describes ever took place remains uncertain.

## Milman Parry

The key to the Homeric Question came in the **1920s and 1930s**, from a young American scholar named **Milman Parry** (1902–1935). Parry noticed that Homer's poetry is built from **formulas**: fixed phrases that recur again and again, fitting the meter of the line. Achilles is "**swift-footed** Achilles"; Odysseus is "**much-enduring** Odysseus" or "**resourceful** Odysseus"; Athena is "**gray-eyed** Athena"; the dawn is "**rosy-fingered** Dawn"; the sea is "**wine-dark**." Parry showed that the choice of epithet depended not on the meaning of the passage but on the metrical position of the name in the line: the poet used whichever formula fitted the meter. He concluded that the poems had been composed by poets who improvised in performance, using a vast stock of traditional formulas inherited from earlier generations.

## Avdo Međedović

To test his theory, Parry traveled in **1933–1935** to **Yugoslavia**, where oral epic poetry was still sung by illiterate bards in the villages and cafés of Bosnia and Montenegro. With his assistant, **Albert Lord**, he recorded thousands of songs on aluminum discs. The greatest singer they found was **Avdo Međedović**, a Muslim farmer and butcher, who, at Parry's request, sang an epic of more than **twelve thousand lines**—as long as the *Odyssey*—over several days. Parry's recordings proved that illiterate poets could compose long, complex epics in performance, using formulas and traditional themes. Parry died in **1935**, at thirty-three, of an accidental gunshot. Lord completed his work in ***The Singer of Tales*** (**1960**).

## Oral-formulaic composition

The **oral-formulaic theory** transformed the study of Homer and of oral literature around the world. It showed that the repetitions in Homer—the recurring epithets, the repeated scenes of arming, feasting, sacrifice, and sailing, the "**type-scenes**"—were not flaws, but essential tools of oral composition. A singer did not memorize a fixed text; he re-created the song each time he performed it, drawing on formulas and themes. Each performance was new, and none was the "original."

How, then, did the *Iliad* and *Odyssey* come to be written down, and in such polished form? Lord suggested that a great oral poet **dictated** them to a scribe, perhaps in the eighth century, when writing became available. Others think the poems continued to evolve orally for generations before being fixed in writing in Athens in the sixth century BCE, under the tyrant **Peisistratus**. The debate continues.

## Rhapsodes and performance

In classical Greece, the Homeric poems were recited by professional performers called **rhapsodes** ("song-stitchers") at festivals, especially the **Panathenaea** in Athens. Plato's dialogue *Ion* portrays one such rhapsode, who weeps when he recites sad passages and boasts of his ability to move audiences. The poems were the foundation of Greek education: children memorized them, philosophers quoted them, and Greeks everywhere regarded them as the common inheritance of their culture.

> **Pause and consider.** Parry showed that Homer's repeated epithets fit the meter rather than the meaning. Does knowing this change how you read "rosy-fingered Dawn"?`,
