      title: "Tell Me About a Complicated Man",
      body: `## The opening

The *Odyssey* opens with an invocation of the **Muse**, the goddess of poetry, who inspires the poet:

> "Tell me, O muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide after he had sacked the famous town of Troy. Many cities did he visit, and many were the nations with whose manners and customs he was acquainted; moreover he suffered much by sea while trying to save his own life and bring his men safely home; but do what he might he could not save his men, for they perished through their own sheer folly in eating the cattle of the Sun-god Hyperion; so the god prevented them from ever reaching home. Tell me, too, about all these things, O daughter of Jove, from whatsoever source you may know them."

This is **Samuel Butler**'s prose translation of **1900**, which is in the public domain. The translation this course recommends is **Emily Wilson**'s (**2017**), the first complete English translation of the *Odyssey* by a woman, which begins: "Tell me about a complicated man." Wilson chose to render the poem in **iambic pentameter**, the meter of Shakespeare and Milton, and to keep the same number of lines as the Greek—12,110—so that her translation would be as swift and concise as the original. (Elsewhere in these lessons, passages are given in the course's own plain prose renderings of the Greek; compare them with Wilson's verse as you read.)

## Polytropos

The first line contains one of the most debated words in Greek literature. The Greek reads: *Andra moi ennepe, Mousa, **polytropon***—"The man, tell me, Muse, the *polytropos* one." *Polytropos* means literally "**many-turned**" or "of many turns." It can mean that Odysseus traveled through many places (he was turned many ways by the winds), that he was clever and versatile (he could turn his mind many ways), or that he was devious and shifty (he turned and twisted the truth). The word captures his complexity.

Translators have rendered it in many ways. **George Chapman** (1616): "the man, O Muse, inform, that many a way / Wound with his wisdom." **Alexander Pope** (1725): "The man for wisdom's various arts renown'd." **Robert Fagles** (1996): "the man of twists and turns." **Robert Fitzgerald** (1961): "that man skilled in all ways of contending." **Richmond Lattimore** (1967): "the man of many ways." Emily Wilson: "**a complicated man**." Wilson explained that she wanted a word that was neither entirely positive nor entirely negative, and that would leave readers to judge Odysseus for themselves. Her choice provoked much discussion; comparing translations of this single word is a lesson in how translators shape what we read.

## He could not save his men

The proem ends on a troubling note: "do what he might he could not save his men." All his companions died on the journey home. The poet blames them for their own fate—they perished "through their own sheer folly" in eating the Sun god's cattle—but the *Odyssey* is the story of a man who returns home alone, having lost every man he led to war. The question of his responsibility for their deaths will run through the poem.

## In medias res

The poem begins, like the *Iliad* and like many later epics, ***in medias res***—"in the middle of things." It is the tenth year after the fall of Troy, and twenty years since Odysseus left home. All the other Greek heroes who survived the war have returned home, or died; Odysseus alone is still away, held captive on the island of **Ogygia** by the nymph **Calypso**, who wants him to be her husband. The gods meet on Mount Olympus, and **Athena**, Odysseus's divine protector, persuades **Zeus** to let him go home. **Poseidon**, the god of the sea, who hates Odysseus for blinding his son, the Cyclops, is away visiting the Ethiopians.

## The structure

The *Odyssey* is divided into **twenty-four books**, a division made by ancient scholars in Alexandria. Its story is told in three main parts:

1. **Books 1–4, the *Telemachy***: Odysseus's son **Telemachus**, now about twenty, sets out to seek news of his father.
2. **Books 5–12**: Odysseus leaves Calypso, is shipwrecked on the island of the **Phaeacians**, and tells them the story of his wanderings since Troy (Books 9–12, his "**Apologue**").
3. **Books 13–24**: Odysseus returns to **Ithaca**, disguised as a beggar, and takes revenge on the **suitors** who have occupied his house.

The most famous adventures—the Cyclops, Circe, the Sirens, Scylla and Charybdis—are told not by the poet but by Odysseus himself, as a flashback, to his Phaeacian hosts. This means that the most fantastic parts of the poem are narrated by a man famous for his lies.

## Athena's role

Throughout the poem, **Athena**, the goddess of wisdom, crafts, and strategy, guides and protects Odysseus and his family. She often appears in disguise—as **Mentes**, a family friend; as **Mentor**, an old Ithacan (from whose name we get the word "mentor"); as a young shepherd; as a little girl. She loves Odysseus, she tells him, because he is like her: clever, cautious, and resourceful. Because he is so courteous, so quick-witted, and so self-controlled, she says, she cannot abandon him in his troubles. The relationship between the goddess and the hero is one of the most charming in the poem.

## Xenia

A central value of the *Odyssey* is ***xenia***, the sacred custom of **hospitality** to strangers. In a world without inns, travelers depended on the generosity of the households they visited. A host was obliged to welcome a stranger, feed him, bathe him, give him a place to sleep, and send him on his way with gifts, *before* asking his name and his business. Hospitality was protected by **Zeus**, "god of strangers," who might punish those who violated it; and the gods themselves sometimes visited human homes in disguise, testing their hosts. Throughout the poem, characters are judged by how they treat guests. The Phaeacians are model hosts; the Cyclops eats his guests; and the suitors, who occupy Odysseus's house, eat his food, and abuse the beggar at his door, are the worst violators of *xenia* of all.

> **Pause and consider.** Translators have called Odysseus "many-turned," "of twists and turns," "skilled in all ways of contending," and "complicated." How does each choice shape your view of him?`,
