      title: "Telemachus and the Suitors",
      body: `## The house of Odysseus

The first book of the *Odyssey* takes us to **Ithaca**, a small, rocky island off the western coast of Greece, where Odysseus was king. In his long absence, his house has been invaded by **108 suitors**, young noblemen from Ithaca and the neighboring islands, who wish to marry his wife, **Penelope**, believing Odysseus dead. For three years, they have occupied the palace, feasting every day on Odysseus's cattle, sheep, pigs, and wine, and sleeping with his maidservants. They insist that Penelope must choose one of them as her husband, and that they will not leave until she does.

## Telemachus

Odysseus's son, **Telemachus**, is about twenty. He was a baby when his father left for Troy. He has grown up in a house dominated by the suitors, without a father to guide him, and he is passive, sad, and unsure of himself. When Athena arrives at the palace, disguised as **Mentes**, an old friend of his father's, Telemachus is sitting among the suitors, "dreaming of his father." He alone notices the stranger at the door, and welcomes him properly, taking his spear and seating him at a table away from the noisy suitors—a sign of his good character and his respect for *xenia*.

Athena tells him that his father is alive, and urges him to take action: to call an assembly, to demand that the suitors leave, and to travel to the mainland to seek news of his father from the old heroes **Nestor** in Pylos and **Menelaus** in Sparta. "You are no child," she tells him; "you must not cling to childhood any longer." When she leaves, flying away like a bird, Telemachus realizes she was a god, and he is transformed: he speaks with new authority to the suitors, and even to his mother.

## Telemachus and Penelope

In a famous scene, the bard **Phemius** is singing to the suitors about the sad homecomings of the Greeks from Troy. Penelope comes down from her room and asks him to sing something else, since the song reminds her of her lost husband. Telemachus rebukes her:

> "Go back to your rooms and see to your own work, the loom and the spindle, and keep the women at their tasks. Speech is the business of men—of all of us, and of me most of all, since the power in this house is mine."

Penelope, "amazed," obeys. The scene is often cited as an example of the poem's patriarchal values, and as a sign of Telemachus's coming of age: he asserts his authority as the man of the house. Emily Wilson has noted that the scene is uncomfortable, and that the poem may invite us to find it so: Telemachus asserts manhood by silencing a woman.

## The assembly

In Book 2, Telemachus calls the first assembly in Ithaca in twenty years, and demands that the suitors leave. The suitors' leader, the arrogant **Antinous**, blames Penelope. For three years, he says, she has deceived them. She told them that she would choose a husband when she had finished weaving a **shroud** for Odysseus's old father, **Laertes**; every day she wove at her great loom, and every night she secretly **unwove** what she had done. After three years, a maid betrayed her, and she was forced to finish it. Antinous declares that the suitors will stay until she marries.

Penelope's trick with the loom is one of the most famous episodes in the poem. It shows that she, like her husband, is clever and capable of deception—*polytropos* in her own way—and that she is, in her own house, fighting her own battle against the suitors with the weapons available to a woman.

## The journey to Pylos and Sparta

Telemachus secretly sails to the mainland with Athena, disguised as Mentor. In **Pylos**, the old king **Nestor**, the wise elder of the *Iliad*, receives him with lavish hospitality and tells him the story of the Greeks' return from Troy, including the murder of **Agamemnon** by his wife **Clytemnestra** and her lover **Aegisthus**, and the revenge taken by Agamemnon's son, **Orestes**. The story of Agamemnon is told repeatedly in the *Odyssey*, as a dark counterpoint to the story of Odysseus: Agamemnon returned home and was murdered by an unfaithful wife; Odysseus will return to a faithful one. Orestes, who avenged his father, is held up as a model for Telemachus.

In **Sparta**, Telemachus visits **Menelaus** and **Helen**, whose elopement with Paris caused the Trojan War, now reconciled and living in luxury. Helen, who recognizes Telemachus by his resemblance to his father, drugs the wine with an Egyptian herb that banishes grief, and tells a story of Odysseus's cleverness at Troy. Menelaus tells how he captured the shape-shifting sea god **Proteus** in Egypt, who revealed that Odysseus was alive, held captive by Calypso.

## The suitors' plot

Meanwhile, in Ithaca, the suitors learn of Telemachus's journey and plot to ambush and kill him on his return. Penelope learns of the plot and despairs, but Athena sends her a comforting dream. The tension between the two halves of the family—the son abroad, the mother at home, the father far away—drives the poem toward their reunion.

## Coming of age

The *Telemachy* is the first story of a young man's **coming of age** in Western literature. Telemachus begins passive and fatherless, and through his journey—his encounters with the old heroes, his experience of hospitality, and the stories he hears of his father—he becomes a man, ready to stand beside Odysseus when he returns. Ancient and modern readers have seen in it a model of education: the young man learns by traveling, listening, and imitating the great. The French archbishop **François Fénelon** expanded it into a novel, ***The Adventures of Telemachus*** (**1699**), a guide for the education of princes, which became one of the most popular books of the eighteenth century.

> **Pause and consider.** Penelope weaves by day and unweaves by night. How is her trick like her husband's cleverness, and how is it different?`,
