      body: `## Ogygia

In Book 5, we meet Odysseus at last. He is on the island of **Ogygia**, "the navel of the sea," with the nymph **Calypso**, whose name means "she who conceals." She rescued him seven years ago, when he was shipwrecked, the last survivor of his crew, and has kept him as her lover ever since. Her island is a paradise: a cave surrounded by a wood of alder, poplar, and cypress, with springs of clear water, meadows of violets and parsley, and a vine heavy with grapes. "Even a god who came there would be amazed and delighted," the poet says.

But Odysseus is miserable. He spends his days sitting on the shore, weeping, gazing at the sea, longing for home:

> He was sitting on the shore, as he always did, wearing out his heart with weeping and groaning, staring across the barren sea with tears in his eyes.

## Hermes' message

Zeus sends **Hermes**, the messenger god, to order Calypso to release Odysseus. Calypso protests bitterly against the double standard of the gods, who punish goddesses for loving mortal men, while male gods take mortal women freely. But she obeys. She goes to Odysseus and tells him that he may go, and that she will help him build a raft.

## The refusal of immortality

Before he leaves, Calypso makes a final offer. She asks him why he wants to return to his mortal wife, when he could stay with her and become **immortal**:

> "If you knew in your heart how much suffering is fated for you before you reach your country, you would stay here with me, keep this house, and never die. … I do not think I am her inferior in body or in bearing. It is not fitting for mortal women to rival goddesses in looks."

Odysseus answers with tact and firmness:

> "Lady goddess, do not be angry with me for this. I know very well that Penelope, for all her good sense, is less to look at than you, in beauty and in stature; she is mortal, and you will never die or grow old. Even so, what I want, and long for every day, is to reach home and see the day of my return. And if some god wrecks me on the wine-dark sea, I will bear it; I have a heart in me that knows how to endure. I have already suffered and toiled through much on the waves and in war; let this be added to the rest."

This is one of the most important moments in the poem. Odysseus is offered the very thing Gilgamesh sought in vain: eternal life and youth. He refuses it. He chooses his **mortal** wife, his rocky island, his aging father, his son, and his own death. The *Odyssey* is, in a sense, a poem about the choice of mortality: about the value of a human life, with its limits, its home, and its relationships, over the empty perfection of the gods. That night, the poet tells us, they went into the cave and made love; and the next day, Odysseus built his raft and sailed away.

## Shipwreck

After seventeen days of sailing, Odysseus sights the island of the **Phaeacians**. But Poseidon, returning from the Ethiopians, sees him and sends a storm that smashes his raft. Odysseus swims for two days and nights, saved by the sea goddess **Ino**, who gives him a magic veil, and finally crawls ashore at the mouth of a river, naked, battered, and exhausted. He covers himself with leaves and falls asleep.

## Nausicaa

In Book 6, Athena sends a dream to **Nausicaa**, the young daughter of the Phaeacian king, telling her to go to the river to wash her clothes, since she will soon be married. Nausicaa and her maids wash the clothes, bathe, and play ball on the shore. Their shouts wake Odysseus, who emerges from the bushes, naked except for a leafy branch held before him, "like a mountain lion." The maids flee; only Nausicaa stands her ground. Odysseus, uncertain whether to clasp her knees as a suppliant, decides it is wiser to keep his distance, and makes a graceful speech, comparing her to a young palm tree he once saw at Delos. Nausicaa gives him clothes and directions to the palace, but tells him to walk in alone, lest people gossip. She is clearly attracted to him; later, she will tell him, "Remember me." The scene is one of the most charming in ancient literature.

## Scheria

The Phaeacians of **Scheria** live in an ideal society, far from other peoples, beloved by the gods. Their king **Alcinous** and queen **Arete** rule wisely; their ships are so fast and so intelligent that they need no steersman, and can find their way anywhere. Their palace gleams with bronze and gold; their orchards bear fruit all year round. Odysseus, still unnamed, is received with perfect hospitality. He is bathed, fed, entertained with athletic games—in which he hurls a discus far beyond the young men who mocked him—and with the songs of the blind bard **Demodocus**.

## The bard's song

Demodocus sings three songs. The second is a comic tale of the love affair of **Ares** and **Aphrodite**, caught in bed by her husband **Hephaestus** in an invisible net, and laughed at by the gods. The first and third are about **Troy**. In the third, at Odysseus's own request, Demodocus sings of the **Trojan Horse**, and of Odysseus's part in the fall of the city. As he listens, Odysseus weeps:

> So Odysseus melted into tears, as a woman weeps, flinging herself on her dear husband, who has fallen before his city and his people, fighting to keep the day of ruin from his town and his children; she sees him gasping as he dies, and clings to him, wailing, while the enemy behind her beat her back and shoulders with their spears and drag her off into slavery, to labor and grief.

The simile is astonishing. Odysseus, the sacker of Troy, weeping at a song of his own triumph, is compared to a **Trojan woman** whose husband has been killed and who is being dragged into slavery—one of the victims of his victory. The poem invites us, through the hero's tears, to feel the suffering of the defeated. Alcinous notices his weeping and asks who he is. And Odysseus, at last, reveals his name: "I am Odysseus, son of Laertes, known to all men for my wiles." Then he begins his story.

> **Pause and consider.** Odysseus refuses Calypso's offer of immortality. Compare his choice with Gilgamesh's quest. What does each poem suggest about the value of mortal life?`,
