      title: "Nobody: The Cyclops",
      body: `## The Apologue

In Books 9–12, Odysseus tells the Phaeacians the story of his wanderings since Troy. These books, called the "**Apologue**" (the tale told to Alcinous), contain the most famous adventures in Western literature: the Lotus-Eaters, the Cyclops, the bag of winds, the cannibal Laestrygonians, Circe, the journey to the underworld, the Sirens, Scylla and Charybdis, and the cattle of the Sun. They are told in the first person, by a man known as a master of lies, to an audience he hopes will send him home with gifts. Some critics have wondered how much of the story we should believe.

## The Lotus-Eaters

After leaving Troy, Odysseus and his twelve ships raided the city of the **Cicones** in Thrace, sacking it and killing the men, and lost many of his men when the Cicones counterattacked. Then a storm drove them far off course, to the land of the **Lotus-Eaters**, who ate the sweet fruit of the lotus plant, which made those who tasted it forget their homes and wish only to stay and eat the lotus forever. Odysseus dragged his men, weeping, back to the ships. The episode is a first temptation: the temptation to forget home.

## The Cyclops

The most famous episode is the adventure with the **Cyclops**, in Book 9. Odysseus and his men land on an island inhabited by the **Cyclopes**, a race of one-eyed giants who live without laws, assemblies, agriculture, or ships, each in his own cave, ruling his own family. They do not plow or plant; the earth produces wheat and grapes for them unbidden. Odysseus describes them as lawless savages, the opposite of civilization.

Odysseus takes twelve men and a skin of strong wine and goes to explore a cave, driven, he admits, by curiosity, and by the hope of receiving gifts of hospitality. The cave belongs to **Polyphemus**, the son of Poseidon. The men find it full of cheeses and lambs, and urge Odysseus to take the food and leave, but he insists on waiting to see the owner.

## The giant

Polyphemus returns with his flock, and closes the cave's entrance with an enormous boulder that twenty-two wagons could not move. He sees the strangers and asks who they are. Odysseus asks for hospitality in the name of Zeus, protector of strangers. Polyphemus laughs: "We Cyclopes do not care about Zeus." Then he seizes two of the men, smashes their heads against the floor "like puppies," and eats them, "like a mountain lion," entrails, flesh, and bones. He is the ultimate violator of *xenia*: a host who eats his guests.

## The plan

Odysseus cannot kill the giant in his sleep, because no one could then move the boulder. He devises a plan. He finds a great olive-wood staff in the cave, sharpens it, and hardens its point in the fire. When Polyphemus returns, Odysseus offers him the strong wine. The Cyclops drinks, and asks his name, promising him a gift. Odysseus replies:

> "Cyclops, you ask my famous name, and I will tell it you. … Nobody is my name. Nobody is what my mother and father call me, and all my companions too."

Polyphemus, drunk, promises: "Then I will eat Nobody last, after his friends. That is my gift to you." And he falls asleep, vomiting wine and pieces of human flesh. Odysseus and his men heat the stake in the fire and drive it into the giant's single eye, twisting it "like a drill," while the eye hisses "like an axe dipped in cold water."

## Nobody is killing me

The blinded Polyphemus screams for help. The other Cyclopes come and ask, through the boulder, who is hurting him. "Nobody is killing me by trickery!" he shouts. "If nobody is hurting you," they reply, "then it must be an illness sent by Zeus; pray to your father Poseidon," and they go away. Odysseus laughs, he says, "at how my name and my clever scheme had tricked him."

The pun works even better in Greek. "Nobody" is *Outis*; but in certain grammatical constructions, the Greek word for "no one" is *mē tis*, which sounds like ***mētis***, "**cunning intelligence**," the quality for which Odysseus is famous. Odysseus's trick depends on the power of language, on wordplay, and on the sacrifice of his own name.

## The rams

The next morning, Polyphemus sits at the cave's entrance, feeling the backs of his sheep as they go out to pasture, to catch the men if they try to escape. Odysseus ties his men beneath the bellies of the rams, three rams to each man, and himself clings beneath the largest ram, the leader of the flock. Polyphemus, stroking the ram's back, speaks to it tenderly, wondering why it is the last to leave, and imagining that it grieves for its master's eye: "If only you could talk." The moment is unexpectedly pathetic: the monster, blind and suffering, is moved by love for his animal. They escape.

## The boast

As his ship pulls away from the shore, Odysseus cannot resist taunting the Cyclops. Despite his men's pleas, he shouts his **real name**:

> "Cyclops, if any mortal man ever asks you who put out your eye and shamed you so, tell him it was Odysseus, sacker of cities, the son of Laertes, whose home is Ithaca."

Polyphemus hurls a huge rock that almost sinks the ship. Then, knowing his enemy's name, he prays to his father **Poseidon**, cursing Odysseus: let him never reach home; or, if he must, let him come late, having lost all his companions, in a stranger's ship, and find trouble in his house. Poseidon hears the prayer. Every word of the curse comes true.

## The cost of a name

The episode reveals the two sides of Odysseus. His cleverness saves him: he becomes "Nobody" to escape. But his pride undoes him: he cannot bear to remain Nobody, and must claim his name and his glory, as a Homeric hero must. In revealing his name, he exposes himself to the god's curse, and dooms his men. The tension between **anonymity** and **fame**, between the cunning that hides and the pride that boasts, runs through the whole poem. Later, Odysseus will return to Ithaca disguised as a nameless beggar, and will win by keeping his name hidden until the right moment.

> **Pause and consider.** Odysseus escapes by calling himself "Nobody," then reveals his name and brings on Poseidon's curse. What does the episode suggest about the relation between cunning and pride?`,
