      title: "Circe and the Land of the Dead",
      body: `## Aeolus and the Laestrygonians

After the Cyclops, Odysseus reaches the floating island of **Aeolus**, king of the winds, who gives him a leather bag containing all the winds except the west wind, which will blow him home. Within sight of Ithaca, while Odysseus sleeps, his men, suspecting the bag contains treasure, open it; the winds rush out and blow them all the way back to Aeolus, who refuses to help them again. Next, they land among the **Laestrygonians**, cannibal giants who destroy eleven of Odysseus's twelve ships with boulders and spear the men like fish. Only Odysseus's own ship escapes.

## Circe

They come to the island of **Aeaea**, home of the goddess **Circe**, an enchantress. A party of Odysseus's men finds her house in the woods, surrounded by wolves and lions that fawn on them like dogs—men she has bewitched. Circe welcomes them, gives them a drink mixed with drugs, and strikes them with her wand, turning them into **pigs**, though they keep their human minds. One man, **Eurylochus**, escapes and warns Odysseus.

On his way to rescue his men, Odysseus meets **Hermes**, who gives him a magic herb called ***moly***, with a black root and a milk-white flower, which will protect him from Circe's drugs. Odysseus drinks her potion without harm, draws his sword, and threatens her. Circe, astonished, recognizes him—Hermes had foretold his coming—and invites him to her bed. He makes her swear not to harm him, and she restores his men to human form, younger and handsomer than before.

## A year with Circe

Odysseus and his men stay with Circe for a full **year**, feasting on meat and wine. It is his men who finally remind him of home. Circe tells him that before he can go home, he must travel to the **land of the dead**, to consult the ghost of the blind prophet **Tiresias**.

Circe has fascinated readers and writers for centuries. She is a dangerous enchantress, who reduces men to beasts, and has been read as a symbol of the sensual pleasures that make men forget their human nature. But she is also a generous host, once tamed, and a wise adviser, who guides Odysseus through the dangers ahead. The novelist **Madeline Miller**, in ***Circe*** (**2018**), retold the story from her point of view, imagining her as a lonely, independent woman, punished by the gods, who turned men into pigs because of what they had done to her.

## Elpenor

The youngest of Odysseus's men, **Elpenor**, drunk, sleeps on Circe's roof on the night before they leave; when he hears the others preparing to sail, he forgets the ladder, falls, and breaks his neck. In their haste, they leave his body unburied. He will be the first ghost Odysseus meets.

## The Nekyia

Book 11, the ***Nekyia*** ("the rite of the dead"), describes Odysseus's visit to the underworld. He sails to the edge of the world, to the land of the **Cimmerians**, shrouded in mist, where the river Ocean flows. There he digs a pit, pours libations of milk, honey, wine, and water, and sacrifices a ram and a black ewe, letting their blood flow into the pit. The ghosts of the dead swarm up from Erebus, "brides, unmarried boys, old men, … young girls," crying out, eager to drink the blood, which will give them the power to speak. Odysseus holds them off with his sword until Tiresias comes.

Notice that Odysseus does not descend into the underworld, as Gilgamesh's Enkidu does in Tablet XII, or as Aeneas will in Virgil's epic; the dead come up to him. The scene is a necromancy, a rite of summoning ghosts.

## Elpenor and Tiresias

First comes **Elpenor**, begging Odysseus to return to Circe's island and bury him, with his oar planted on his grave. Odysseus promises. Then comes **Tiresias**, who drinks the blood and prophesies. He tells Odysseus that Poseidon is angry with him; that he must not harm the cattle of the Sun on the island of **Thrinacia**, or he will lose his ship and his men; that he will return home late, alone, in a stranger's ship, and find suitors in his house, whom he will kill. And then Tiresias gives a strange prophecy: after killing the suitors, Odysseus must take an oar and walk inland until he reaches a people who know nothing of the sea, who mistake the oar for a winnowing fan; there he must sacrifice to Poseidon. And death will come to him "from the sea," or perhaps "far from the sea," gently, in a ripe old age, among a prosperous people.

## His mother

Then Odysseus sees the ghost of his **mother**, **Anticleia**, whom he had left alive when he sailed for Troy. She tells him that she died of grief for him. Three times he tries to embrace her, and three times she slips through his arms "like a shadow or a dream." She explains that this is the nature of the dead: "the sinews no longer hold the flesh and bones together; the fire destroys them, and the spirit flies away like a dream." The scene, echoing the futile embrace of Enkidu's ghost in Gilgamesh's Tablet XII, would be imitated by Virgil and Dante.

## The heroines and the heroes

Odysseus then sees a parade of famous women of legend, and then the heroes of the Trojan War. **Agamemnon** tells him of his murder by Clytemnestra, and warns him not to trust even Penelope: "There is no trust in women." But he admits that Penelope is wise and faithful.

## Achilles

The most famous encounter is with **Achilles**, the greatest hero of the *Iliad*, who chose a short life of glory over a long life without fame. Odysseus greets him with flattery: in life, Achilles was honored like a god; now he rules among the dead. Achilles' answer is one of the most startling passages in Greek literature:

> "Do not try to make light of death to me, shining Odysseus. I would rather work the land as a hired laborer for another man, some poor farmer with little to live on, than be lord over all the dead who have perished."

The greatest of heroes, who chose glory over life, now says that he would rather be the lowest of the living than the king of the dead. The heroic ideal of the *Iliad*—fame at the cost of life—is overturned. Like Gilgamesh learning that fame does not defeat death, Achilles learns that glory is no consolation in the house of the dead. The passage confirms Odysseus's choice on Calypso's island: life, any life, is better than death. Achilles asks only for news of his son, **Neoptolemus**; told that the young man fought bravely at Troy, he strides away across the meadow of asphodel, rejoicing.

## Ajax

Odysseus also meets the ghost of **Ajax**, who had committed suicide after losing the contest for Achilles' armor to Odysseus. Odysseus speaks to him gently, wishing he had never won the prize. Ajax does not answer; he turns away in silence and goes back into the darkness. The ancient critic **Longinus** praised this silence as more sublime than any speech.

> **Pause and consider.** Achilles says he would rather be a hired laborer on earth than king of all the dead. How does this reverse the heroic values of the *Iliad*, and how does it echo *Gilgamesh*?`,
