      title: "Sirens, Scylla, and the Cattle of the Sun",
      body: `## The Sirens

Returning to Circe's island, Odysseus buries Elpenor, and Circe warns him of the dangers ahead. The first is the **Sirens**, two creatures (often depicted in art as birds with women's heads) who sit in a meadow surrounded by the bones of sailors, and sing so sweetly that anyone who hears them forgets his home and stays until he dies. Following Circe's advice, Odysseus plugs his men's ears with beeswax, and has himself tied to the mast, so that he can hear the song without being able to act on it. When the ship passes, the Sirens sing:

> "Come here, Odysseus, much-praised, great glory of the Achaeans; stop your ship and listen to our voice. No one has ever sailed past here in his black ship without hearing the honey-sweet song from our lips, and he goes on his way delighted and knowing more. For we know all the toils that the Greeks and Trojans endured in wide Troy by the will of the gods, and we know everything that happens on the fruitful earth."

What the Sirens offer Odysseus is not sexual pleasure, as later interpretations often assumed, but **knowledge**—above all, knowledge of the Trojan War, the story of his own glory. They offer him the epic itself. Odysseus begs his men to release him, but they tie him tighter, and the ship sails past.

The episode has been interpreted endlessly. The philosophers **Theodor Adorno** and **Max Horkheimer**, in *Dialectic of Enlightenment* (1944), read it as an allegory of modern civilization: the master, Odysseus, enjoys art, but bound and powerless to act on it, while the workers, their ears stopped, row on without hearing. The Irish novelist **James Joyce** made the Sirens a chapter of *Ulysses* (1922), set in a Dublin bar, full of music.

## Scylla and Charybdis

Next, the ship must pass between two dangers in a narrow strait: **Scylla**, a monster with six heads on long necks, each with three rows of teeth, who lives in a cave in a cliff and seizes sailors from passing ships; and **Charybdis**, a whirlpool that sucks down the sea three times a day and spews it out again. Circe has advised Odysseus to sail closer to Scylla, and accept the loss of six men, rather than risk the whole ship in Charybdis. She has also told him not to fight Scylla, since she is immortal and cannot be defeated. Odysseus, characteristically, arms himself anyway, but it does no good. Scylla snatches six men from the deck:

> They cried out and called me by name for the last time, in the anguish of their hearts. … There at her door she devoured them, shrieking, stretching out their hands to me in that dreadful struggle. Of all the things I suffered searching out the paths of the sea, that was the most pitiful my eyes have seen.

"Between Scylla and Charybdis" became a proverb for a choice between two evils.

## Thrinacia

Then they come to the island of **Thrinacia**, where the cattle of **Helios**, the Sun God, graze. Both Tiresias and Circe had warned Odysseus that his men must not harm them. Odysseus wants to sail past, but his men, exhausted, insist on landing for the night, led by **Eurylochus**. Odysseus makes them swear not to touch the cattle. Then contrary winds keep them on the island for a month. Their food runs out. They hunt fish and birds, and starve.

One day, while Odysseus has gone inland to pray, and has fallen asleep, Eurylochus persuades the men to kill the cattle: "All deaths are hateful to us wretched mortals, but the most pitiful way to die is starvation." They kill and roast the best of the cattle, and when Odysseus wakes and smells the roasting meat, he cries out in horror. Portents follow: the hides crawl, and the meat on the spits bellows like living cattle.

## The last shipwreck

Helios complains to Zeus, threatening to go down and shine among the dead if he is not avenged. When the winds change and the men sail, Zeus strikes the ship with a thunderbolt. It breaks apart, and all the men drown. Only Odysseus, who had not eaten the cattle, survives, clinging to the mast and keel. He is swept back to **Charybdis**, where he escapes by clinging to a fig tree above the whirlpool until it spews out his mast; he drifts for nine days, and on the tenth reaches **Ogygia**, Calypso's island, where his story, and Book 5, began.

## He could not save them

The cattle of the Sun episode fulfills the proem's statement that Odysseus's men perished "through their own sheer folly," because they ate the Sun god's cattle. The poem seems to blame the men, and to absolve Odysseus. But a careful reader may wonder. It was Odysseus's curiosity that led them into the Cyclops's cave, where six men died; his boast that brought down Poseidon's curse; his sleep that let the men open the bag of winds and kill the cattle. The poem gives us the materials for a more critical judgment of the "complicated man." His men, starving, faced a terrible choice. The ancient tradition of epic praise and the modern tradition of skeptical reading pull in different directions, and the poem allows both.

## The end of the tale

Odysseus ends his story. The Phaeacians sit "spellbound" in the shadowy hall. Alcinous praises his storytelling: there is grace in his words and good sense within, and he has told his tale with the skill of a bard. The comparison is pointed: Odysseus is not only a hero but a **poet**, a master storyteller, and the most fantastic adventures of the *Odyssey* are his own composition. The Phaeacians load him with gifts and send him home on one of their magical ships, and he falls into a deep sleep "like death." When he wakes, he is on Ithaca.

> **Pause and consider.** The Sirens offer Odysseus knowledge of the Trojan War—the story of his own glory. Why might such knowledge be fatal?`,
